This approach follows the SOLID principle of Single Responsibility (base class handles common attributes, derived class handles specific behavior) and minimizes memory usage by using simple data types and avoiding unnecessary object creation.

## Introduction to TypeScript Advanced Types
TypeScript provides advanced type features that allow developers to model complex data structures with precision. Among these, union types and intersection types are particularly powerful. Union types allow a variable to be of multiple types, while intersection types combine multiple types into one. Together, they enable the creation of flexible and robust type systems.

## Union Types
Union types are defined using the pipe symbol (`|`). They allow a variable to be of one of several types. For example, a variable can be either a string or a number. This is useful when a function can accept multiple types of arguments.

```typescript
function example(value: string | number) {
  if (typeof value === 'string') {
    console.log('String value:', value);
  } else {
    console.log('Number value:', value);
  }
}
```

## Intersection Types
Intersection types are defined using the `&` symbol. They combine multiple types into one, allowing a variable to have all the properties of the combined types. This is useful when you want to merge multiple interfaces or types.

```typescript
interface User {
  name: string;
}

interface Role {
  role: string;
}

type UserWithRole = User & Role;

const user: UserWithRole = {
  name: 'Alice',
  role: 'Admin'
};
```

## Combining Union and Intersection Types
Union and intersection types can be used together to model complex data structures. For example, a function can accept a parameter that is either an object with specific properties or a string.

```typescript
function processInput(input: string | { id: number; name: string }) {
  if (typeof input === 'string') {
    console.log('String input:', input);
  } else {
    console.log('Object input:', input.id, input.name);
  }
}
```

## Impact on Code Maintainability and Readability
Using union and intersection types improves code maintainability and readability by making the intentions of the code explicit. Developers can understand the expected types of variables and parameters, reducing the likelihood of type-related errors. Additionally, these types allow for more precise type checking, which helps catch errors at compile time rather than runtime.

## Conclusion
TypeScript's union and intersection types are powerful tools for modeling complex data structures. By combining these types, developers can create flexible and robust type systems that enhance code maintainability and readability. Understanding how to use these types effectively is essential for writing high-quality TypeScript code.

The BroadcastReceiver.onReceive method in Android SDK is used to handle system-wide broadcast events and receive updates from other apps. It is available from API level 1 (Android 1.0) and runs on the main thread by default. Best practices include using manifest-declared receivers for system-wide broadcasts and dynamically registering/unregistering receivers for specific use cases. Manifest-declared receivers are useful for system-wide events, while dynamic registration allows for more flexibility and control. Example code snippet:

// Manifest-declared receiver
<receiver android:name=".MyBroadcastReceiver">
    <intent-filter>
        <action android:name="android.intent.action.BOOT_COMPLETED" />
    </intent-filter>
</receiver>

// Dynamic registration in code
IntentFilter filter = new IntentFilter("com.example.MY_CUSTOM_ACTION");
MyBroadcastReceiver receiver = new MyBroadcastReceiver();
registerReceiver(receiver, filter);

// Unregistering the receiver
unregisterReceiver(receiver);

Execution constraints include running on the main thread, which means that long-running operations should be avoided. The onReceive method should complete quickly to prevent application freezing. Security considerations involve setting the exported status of the receiver to true if it needs to receive broadcasts from other apps. This requires appropriate permissions to be declared in the manifest, such as android.permission.RECEIVE_BOOT_COMPLETED for system-wide events. It is important to ensure that the receiver is not exposed unnecessarily to avoid security vulnerabilities.

